voices at capacity:
  a chain is tried in order: when VoxCPM2 is at capacity (every instance at its limit) or down, the sentence waits briefly (VOXCPM_QUEUE_S, 2 s) for a free instance, then goes to the next voice (languages with no voice after VoxCPM2 wait up to VOXCPM_WAIT_S, 15 s); eSpeak NG (last resort) ends every chain it speaks, if espeak-ng is installed
  VoxCPM2 speaks 10 languages (ar, id, km, ko, lo, ru, sw, tl, tr, vi); one instance makes ~2-3 real-time voices, and takes up to 1 sentences at once (VOXCPM_MAX_INFLIGHT)
  VoxCPM2 instances: auto (the commercial default): one on the main GPU plus one on each other GPU with ≥ 10 GB, up to 4 — depends on the GPUs at start (VOXCPM_INSTANCES, VOXCPM_GPUS)
  on this machine now: VoxCPM2: 1 instance (one GPU); overflow → fallback voices
summary (commercial):
  text only: none
  eSpeak NG only (robotic, better than text): fa, ro
  attribution / share-alike you must honour (e.g. on an About screen):
    - bn piper bn_BD-google-medium: CC-BY-SA 4.0: credit Google's OpenSLR 37 Bengali corpus and keep the CMU notice
    - de piper de_DE-mls-medium: CC-BY 4.0: credit Multilingual LibriSpeech (Pratap et al., 2020)
    - fr piper fr_FR-mls-medium: CC-BY 4.0: credit Multilingual LibriSpeech (Pratap et al., 2020)
    - ht coqui multilingual-tts/VITS-OpenBible-Haitian-Creole: CC-BY-SA 4.0: credit multilingual-tts / Open Bible; share adaptations of the model under CC-BY-SA
    - it piper it_IT-serena-high: CC-BY 4.0: credit the serena-synthetic-it-27h dataset (committa)
  deployment-wide, 21 languages: espeak: GPL-3.0: nothing to do for running the stack or for the audio; if you distribute the image or a volume, include espeak-ng's licence and its source (docs/licences.md §8)
  libraries: espeak-ng, piper-tts and phonemizer-fork are GPL-3.0, coqui-tts MPL-2.0 — no duty for running the stack as a service; if you distribute the image or a volume, follow GPL-3.0 for them (docs/licences.md §8)
